  NOTE: user-secrets only loads when ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=Development.

Step 4. Add a development-only sign-in switch (the one code change)
Why: the API only accepts a Cloudflare-signed Cf-Access-Jwt-Assertion token and nothing local produces one,
so every call would be 401. Keep production exactly as strict.
Rules for the change:
- Lives in Authentication/ only (AccessAuthenticationHandler and AccessAuthenticationExtensions). No new files or folders.
- New optional setting Access:DevUserEmail. It is honoured ONLY when IHostEnvironment.IsDevelopment() is true,
  checked in code at registration/handling time, not just by config. In any other environment the setting is
  ignored and a request without a valid token is still rejected.
- When active and no Cf-Access-Jwt-Assertion header is present: authenticate as that email (name = part before @).
  A request that DOES carry a token is still fully verified.
- Log one warning at startup when the switch is active.
- Tests (names <Condition>_<Method>_<Expected>):
  * unit: switch ignored when environment is Production, even if DevUserEmail is set
  * unit/integration: Development + DevUserEmail + no token -> authenticated as that email
  * integration: Production-like host with no token -> 401
- Do not put DevUserEmail in appsettings.json; set it with user-secrets:
  dotnet user-secrets set "Access:DevUserEmail" "<the developer's own email>"
CHECK: dotnet test AI.Client.slnx all pass, including the three new tests.

